= Condemned to Rule

As the dispute with the military prosecutors escalates, Muhsin Batur changes his stance. While he once supported İnönü and agreed with his view that the accusations were unjust, he now joins forces with Memduh Tağmaç, Faruk Gürler, and Celal Eyiceoğlu. All four commanders agree that they will not act against the prosecutors, and so İnönü's demands go unanswered and Erim's efforts to mediate break down. It is clear that the military has taken a firm stance and will not back down.

Erim becomes very frustrated. Unable to mediate between İnönü and the generals, and increasingly frustrated by his inability to exercise control over military institutions supposedly operating under his government, he submits his resignation as Prime Minister on March 27, before the martial law authorities issue their public statement. He feels that the balance between the military, political parties, and the presidency upon which he based his government is no longer sustainable. However, Cevdet Sunay does not accept his resignation, as he believes it would cause further political problems if the Prime Minister were to leave. Despite losing much faith in the arrangement that brought him to power, Erim has little choice but to continue as Prime Minister.

Things worsen further. On March 31, the Ankara Martial Law Command issues a public statement in response to İnönü's allegations. The military authorities state that they completely disagree with the claims made by the CHP, maintaining that the military prosecutors did nothing wrong. As İnönü has previously criticized these accusations in Parliament, he is now in direct conflict with the martial law authorities. Moreover, the very military whose political involvement he had reluctantly accepted in order to preserve parliamentary democracy appears increasingly unwilling to tolerate criticism from the man who helped found the Republic.

